\textbf{\textcolor{red}{AIMS OF TIME SERIES ANALYSIS}}

$\to$ \textbf{Describe} -- trend, seasonal, cycle (Often, to de-trend, de-seasonalize). If multivariate: find common patterns, clusters, dim. reductions etc... 

$\to$ \textbf{Explain} 
- modeling and inference write a model and estimate its parameters
- \textbf{Forecast} accounting for uncertainty

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FITTING A TREND}}

For $Y_t = T_t + E_t$ (no seasonal comp.):

\textbf{1. OLS.} 
\textbf{2. Moving averages.} e.g.\ MA of order $k=3$:
\[ \widehat{T}_t = \frac{y_{t-1} + y_t + y_{t+1}}{3},\quad t = 2, 3, \dots \]
If $k$ is even, e.g.\ $k = 4$:
\[ \widehat{T}_t = \frac{0.5\, y_{t-2} + y_{t-1} + y_t + y_{t+1} + 0.5\, y_{t+2}}{4}. \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{DETRENDING}}
\[ y_t^{\text{detrended}} = y_t - \widehat{T}_t. \]

after you detrend, you remove the seasonal coponent

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FORECASTING ALGORITHMS — CASES 0, 1, 2}}

Predict $y_{t+1}\mid y_{1:t}$ — often the ultimate goal. Build the forecast progressively, adding one component at each step.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Case 0 — no trend, no seasonality $\to$ Simple Exponential Smoothing (SES).}}

Weighted average with geometrically decreasing weights (not a regression: parameters are fixed, not estimated):\[ \hat{y}_{t+1\mid t} = \alpha\, y_t + \alpha(1-\alpha)\, y_{t-1} + \alpha(1-\alpha)^2\, y_{t-2} + \dots + \alpha(1-\alpha)^{t-1}\, y_1. \]Smoothing parameter $0 < \alpha < 1$; weights sum approx.\ to 1 for large $t$.

\textbf{Recursive form:}\[ L_t = \alpha\, y_t + (1-\alpha)\, L_{t-1}, \qquad \hat y_{t+h\mid t} = L_t. \]\textbf{Forecast-error correction form:}\[ \hat{y}_{t+1\mid t} = \hat{y}_{t\mid t-1} + \alpha\,(y_t - \hat{y}_{t\mid t-1}). \]Forecast is flat: only the current level is propagated forward.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Case 1 — add trend $\to$ Holt.}}

\[ L_t = \alpha\, y_t + (1-\alpha)\,(L_{t-1} + B_{t-1}) \]\[ B_t = \beta\,(L_t - L_{t-1}) + (1-\beta)\, B_{t-1} \]\[ \hat y_{t+h\mid t} = L_t + B_t\, h. \]$L_t$ tracks the level; $B_t$ tracks the slope (growth). Note $L_{t-1} + B_{t-1} = \hat y_{t\mid t-1}$, so this is the SES update applied to the level component, with an extra recursion for the slope. Forecast is a line.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Case 2 — add seasonality $\to$ Holt-Winters.}}

E.g.\ monthly data, $m = 12$. The seasonal factor satisfies $S_t \approx S_{t+m}$.
\[ L_t = \alpha\,(y_t - S_{t-m}) + (1-\alpha)\,(L_{t-1} + B_{t-1}) \]\[ B_t = \beta\,(L_t - L_{t-1}) + (1-\beta)\, B_{t-1} \]\[ S_t = \gamma\,(y_t - L_{t-1} - B_{t-1}) + (1-\gamma)\, S_{t-m} \]\[ \hat y_{t+h\mid t} = L_t + B_t\, h + S_{t+h-m}. \]Three smoothing parameters $\alpha, \beta, \gamma \in (0,1)$ for level, growth, season. Tune them jointly by grid search (MAE / MSE / MAPE on held-out data).

\textbf{Picture:} the same series is shown three times — SES gives a flat forecast (misses trend and season); Holt captures the trend but rides the wrong slope at the boundary because of unmodelled seasonality; Holt-Winters captures all three components.

\textit{Counterexample (visual):}

The $Y_t$ may have the same mean $\mu(t) = \mu$ and constant variance, but the \emph{marginal distributions} of $Y_1$ and $Y_t$ may be different.

$\Rightarrow$ stationary $\not\Rightarrow$ strictly stationary.

\textbf{Markov property:}
\[ p(y_t \mid y_0, y_1, \dots, y_{t-1}) = p(y_t \mid y_{t-1}),\quad \forall t \ge 1. \]

\textbf{Practically:} to assign the finite-dim.\ distributions of a Markov chain, enough to specify
\begin{itemize} \item the initial distribution $p_0(\cdot)$ of $Y_0$, \item the transition probabilities $p_{i,j}(t) = P(Y_t = j \mid Y_{t-1} = i),\ i, j \in \mathcal{Y}$. \end{itemize}

\textit{See: indep.\ of time!} $\to$ \textbf{Homogeneous Markov chain.}

trash.content:
SES $\to$ no result to track predictive intensity. \textit{Need to express it as Stoch.\ Process.}

$\to$ Multi-dimensional $P_t$.

Defining $\to$ statistical model has to specify all (and only) the assumptions, allowing to write the joint law of the stoch.\ process $(Y_t)$, i.e.\ write
\[ P(Y_1,\dots,Y_n) \]
for any $n$ and at all times $t_1,\dots,t_n$. \textit{Stoch.\ process.}

\textbf{Tuning} $\alpha$:
\begin{itemize} \item consider a grid of values $\alpha \in (0,1)$ \item for each $\alpha$, compute one-step-ahead forecasts for $t = 1,\dots,T$ \item ``score'' the forecasts; pick $\alpha$ giving best predictive performance \end{itemize}

Popular scores: MAE, MSE, MAPE (scale-free).
